WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 151: Grover Cleveland: The Veto President and the Triumph of 'Grover the Good'

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.680
[Rebecca] Today we step into the Gilded Age to examine the extraordinary rise of Grover Cleveland.

4
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.840
[Rebecca] He was an unpolished, mountainous man of immense physical stamina and unbending moral certainty.

5
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:29.680
[Rebecca] In an era dominated by backroom bribes and political kickbacks, Cleveland stood like an immovable granite boulder, telling the American people that the government was not an automated dispenser of favors.

6
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:43.120
[George] Few American presidents ever entered public life with a background as unvarnished or physically formidable as Stephen Grover Cleveland.

8
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:57.440
[George] Born in 1837 in Caldwell, New Jersey, the fifth of nine children of a poor Presbyterian clergyman, Cleveland experienced abrupt tragedy when his father died suddenly when Grover was just 16 years old.

9
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:11.680
[George] Forced to abandon dreams of higher education, the young man worked long hours as an assistant instructor in a New York City institution for the blind before boarding an eerie canal packet boat headed west, intending to start over in Ohio.

10
00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:18.240
[George] But when his boat docked in the bustling lake port of Buffalo, an uncle convinced him to stay and read law.

11
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:32.320
[George] Cleveland threw himself into his legal studies with ferocious intensity, earning admission to the bar and quickly establishing a reputation as a relentless, incorruptible legal workhorse who could outwork every attorney in Western New York.

12
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:38.160
[George] In 1870, at age 33, Cleveland was elected sheriff of Erie County.

13
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:45.680
[George] Under the laws of the era, the county sheriff was legally required to personally supervise and execute convicted murderers.

14
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:55.120
[George] Most sheriffs found the duty so distasteful that they paid a deputy $10 to pull the trapdoor at hangings to avoid public stigma and moral squeamishness.

15
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:06.840
[George] But Grover Cleveland believed that if the law imposed an unpleasant duty on an elected official, that official had a sacred moral obligation to perform it himself rather than delegate it to a subordinate.

16
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:16.520
[George] On two separate occasions, Sheriff Grover Cleveland walked onto the wooden gallows in the jail yard and personally tripped the lever that hanged convicted murderers.

17
00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:22.920
[George] When squeamish Buffalo citizens expressed horror, Cleveland replied with calm, stony resolve.

18
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.120
[George] The law commanded it, and I was sworn to execute the law without fear or favor.

19
00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:44.120
[George] Backslash N backslash N as sheriff, Cleveland also reformed county jail conditions, personally inspecting prisoner rations, ending corrupt kickbacks between deputies and bail bondsmen, and establishing strict accounting for municipal property.

20
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:59.640
[George] By the late 1870s, Buffalo was in the grip of rampant municipal corruption, with corrupt contractors and pliable city aldermen systematically looting the city treasury through fraudulent street cleaning, sewer construction, and paving contracts.

21
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:07.960
[George] In 1881, reform-minded civic leaders approached Cleveland, urging him to run for mayor on an independent reform ticket.

22
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:14.680
[George] Cleveland accepted, won the election by a wide margin, and immediately revolutionized city government.

23
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:23.880
[George] He scrutinized every municipal contract, slapping dozens of corrupt awards with stinging vetoes and boldly declaring in public messages.

24
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.680
[George] This is a brazen, unblushing attempt to plunder the taxpayers of Buffalo.

25
00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.440
[George] And I will not permit it.

26
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:43.320
[George] His unbending crusade against Graft earned him the affectionate title, Grover the Good, and catapulted him to the governorship of New York in 1882 by a record-breaking majority of nearly 200,000 votes.

27
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:51.840
[George] In Albany, Governor Cleveland refused to take orders from Tammany Hall, the legendary Democratic political machine of New York City.

28
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.720
[George] When Tammany boss honest John Kelly demanded lucrative patronage appointments, Cleveland refused to see him.

29
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:09.440
[George] He vetoed special interest bills favored by Wall Street tycoons, railroad monopolies, and labor unions alike whenever he believed they conflicted with the state constitution.

30
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.680
[George] His fame spread across the nation as a man of rock solid integrity who answered only to his conscience.

31
00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:32.240
[George] Backslash N backslash N when local ward bosses attempted to slip unbudgeted expenditures past him under the guise of public celebrations, Cleveland struck them down with caustic vetoes, insisting that public funds were held in sacred trust for taxpayers alone.

32
00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:41.120
[George] When the Democratic National Convention convened in Chicago in 1884, delegates nominated Cleveland for president on the second ballot.

33
00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.800
[George] General Edward S.

34
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.840
[George] Bragg of Wisconsin delivered an immortal tribute that defined Cleveland's appeal.

35
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:54.400
[George] They love him, gentlemen, and they respect him, not only for himself, but for the enemies he has made.

36
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.600
[George] Cleveland faced Republican nominee James G.

37
00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:07.120
[George] Blaine, the brilliant, charismatic orator from Maine, whose career was heavily tarnished by financial corruption revealed in the infamous Mulligan Letters.

38
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.920
[George] Just as the general election campaign got underway, a sensational scandal exploded in the national press.

39
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:26.400
[George] A Buffalo newspaper revealed that ten years earlier, Bachelor Grover Cleveland had fathered an illegitimate child with a widow named Maria Halpin, and had paid for the child's care and eventual adoption.

40
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:35.160
[George] Republicans seized upon the revelation with gleeful savagery, marching through city streets chanting, Ma, Ma, where's my pa?

41
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:43.080
[George] Going to the White House, ha ha ha, while his terrified political managers urged him to issue denials or blame other men.

42
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.200
[George] Cleveland sent a historic two-word telegram that electrified American politics.

43
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.480
[George] Tell the truth.

44
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:59.160
[George] Cleveland candidly admitted his past, accepted full moral and financial responsibility, and refused to utter a single derogatory word about the mother.

45
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:05.960
[George] His radical honesty disarmed voters and drew a stark contrast with Blaine's evasive denials of bribery.

46
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:18.040
[George] Backslash and backslash and Cleveland's refusal to lie resonated with independent voters known as mugwumps, reform Republicans who abandoned Blaine's corrupt candidacy to back the straight talking Democrat.

47
00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:25.800
[George] In the final week of the 1884 campaign, the election turned on a monumental unforced error by the Republican campaign.

48
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:32.920
[George] At a lavish reception held for Blaine at the Fifth Avenue Hotel in New York, a prominent Protestant clergyman named Dr.

49
00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:33.960
[George] Samuel D.

50
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.560
[George] Burchard delivered an impassioned endorsement speech.

51
00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:50.120
[George] Burchard proclaimed that the Democratic Party was the party whose antecedents were rum, Romanism, and rebellion, a vicious, bigoted slur aimed directly at the Catholic Church and Irish American voters.

52
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.800
[George] Blaine, exhausted from traveling, failed to hear the remark or rebuke the speaker.

53
00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:08.040
[George] Democratic operatives moved with lightning speed, printing hundreds of thousands of flyers quoting the insult and distributing them outside Catholic churches across New York City the very next Sunday morning.

54
00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.960
[George] On election day, enraged Irish Catholic voters flocked to the polls in historic numbers.

55
00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:25.680
[George] Cleveland carried New York State by a razor thin margin of just 1,047 votes out of more than one million cast, capturing its 36 electoral votes and securing the presidency.

56
00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:34.800
[George] When jubilant Democrats filled the streets that night, they shouted a triumphant answer to the Republican taunt Ma, where's my pa.

57
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:48.240
[George] Gone to the White House, ha ha, ha backslash n backslash n in his victory statement, Cleveland declared that the presidency belonged to no party or faction, promising to govern with strict impartiality.

58
00:07:48.240 --> 00:08:00.400
[George] Entering the executive mansion in March 1885 as the first Democratic president since James Buchanan, Cleveland brought the unrelenting work ethic of an Erie County Sheriff to Washington.

59
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:15.520
[George] A massive, barrel-chested man weighing nearly 260 pounds, Cleveland routinely worked from eight in the morning until two or three o'clock in the morning, answering correspondence by hand and personally reading every single bill passed by Congress.

60
00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:21.440
[George] What followed was the most extraordinary exercise of executive veto power in American history.

61
00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:28.160
[George] In the late 19th century, Congress had turned the Civil War pension system into a corrupt political racket.

62
00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:41.360
[George] Congressmen introduced thousands of private pension bills awarding taxpayer money to fraudulent claimants, men who had deserted their units, suffered injuries years after the war, or never served in uniform at all.

63
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:50.240
[George] While previous presidents had routinely signed these bills by the thousands without examination, Cleveland personally inspected every pension docket.

64
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:55.360
[George] When he discovered fraud, he struck the bill down with sarcastic veto messages.

65
00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:07.560
[George] In one veto, rejecting a claim for a soldier who claimed a war injury from a fall, Cleveland dryly noted that the man had broken his leg when thrown from a buggy while on leave visiting a local tavern.

66
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:26.600
[George] In his first term alone, Cleveland vetoed 414 bills, more than all previous 21 presidents combined, backslash and backslash and congressmen who dared to complain were publicly invited to review the pension records alongside the president, an offer few corrupt politicians dared to accept.

67
00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:32.120
[George] Cleveland's most enduring philosophical statement occurred in February 1887.

68
00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:42.840
[George] A catastrophic, multi-year drought had devastated farmers across the plains of western Texas, ruining crops and leaving thousands of homestead families on the brink of starvation.

69
00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:53.000
[George] Congress passed an emergency relief bill appropriating $10,000, a modest sum, to purchase and distribute seed grain to the suffering Texas farmers.

70
00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:00.040
[George] The bill was widely popular, and political advisers urged Cleveland to sign it as an easy humanitarian gesture.

71
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:04.360
[George] Cleveland vetoed the bill with unyielding constitutional conviction.

72
00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:11.720
[George] In his message, Cleveland articulated a fundamental principle that became a cornerstone of limited government philosophy.

73
00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.880
[George] I can find no warrant for such an appropriation in the Constitution.

74
00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:26.520
[George] And I do not believe that the power and duty of the general government ought to be extended to the relief of individual suffering, which is in no manner properly related to the public service.

75
00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:40.680
[George] A prevalent tendency to disregard the limited mission of this power should be steadfastly resisted, to the end that the lesson should be constantly enforced that, though the people support the government, the government should not support the people.

76
00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:48.320
[George] Cleveland argued that private charity and voluntary associations, not federal paternalism, should aid the distressed.

77
00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:54.000
[George] Within weeks, private American citizens and philanthropic groups responded generously.

78
00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.880
[George] Raising over four times more money for Texas farmers than Congress had proposed.

79
00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:10.640
[George] Backslash and backslash and Cleveland's veto established a national precedent that federal assistance was never an entitlement, challenging the emerging culture of federal paternalism in the Gilded Age.

80
00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.920
[George] Cleveland's first administration transformed the standing of the American presidency.

81
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:29.440
[George] He defended the civil service against tens of thousands of hungry Democratic office seekers who had waited 24 years for federal jobs, infuriating party bosses by protecting competent Republican clerks under the Pendleton Act.

82
00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:41.120
[George] He signed the Landmark Interstate Commerce Act of 1887, establishing the first Federal Regulatory Commission to curb the abusive rate-fixing practices of railroad robber barons.

83
00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:57.760
[George] Furthermore, Cleveland ordered the investigation and recovery of over 81 million acres of public lands in the West that had been fraudulently seized by timber syndicates, cattle barons, and railroad monopolies, returning the acreage to ordinary American homesteaders.

84
00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:02.960
[George] Cleveland was blunt, stubborn, and completely indifferent to political applause.

85
00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:12.960
[George] He believed that the presidency was not an instrument for courting popularity, but a solemn sacred trust to defend the taxpayer and the Constitution against organized greed.

86
00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:22.240
[George] As an admiring contemporary historian wrote, Grover Cleveland was an honest man who said no to America when America desperately needed to hear it.

87
00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.680
[George] Backslash and backslash and by the end of his term.

88
00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:32.520
[George] Cleveland had demonstrated that honest executive administration could triumph over partisan machine politics.

89
00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:40.040
[Rebecca] Grover Cleveland's first administration restored independence and fiscal rectitude to the executive mansion.

90
00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:45.640
[Rebecca] His unbending refusal to pander to special interests made him a legend of moral fortitude.

91
00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:50.040
[Rebecca] Next week, we examine the dramatic turning point of Cleveland's life.

92
00:12:50.040 --> 00:13:00.680
[Rebecca] His romantic White House wedding to the captivating Francis Folsom, his fearless assault on the protective tariff, and his dramatic defeat in the election of 1888.

93
00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:05.000
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

